# ThreadSanitizer suppressions for the panel-on leg (CI: "TSan with Qt").
#
# The leg runs Qt on its offscreen platform with the glib dispatcher off, so no xcb, X11, D-Bus
# or glib is in the sanitised process at all - those are uninstrumented, built without frame
# pointers, and their own mutex use confuses the sanitiser's model beyond any suppression's
# reach. What remains uninstrumented is Qt itself. Its hand-offs to and from our threads (the
# blocking queued invokes that build and delete a window on the Qt thread) are made visible to
# the sanitiser by the acquire/release atomics in src/panel/panel.cpp, so nothing about them is
# suppressed here. What is: reports whose stacks run through Qt's own libraries, and what those
# libraries call into libc - Qt's internal synchronisation is futex-based and invisible, so a
# report with a Qt frame in it is a report about Qt, not about the seam. The seam
# (WormLib::Mailbox) is a std::mutex the sanitiser sees whole, and a race there is real.

# What Qt's libraries call into libc (memcpy, strlen, pthread) is theirs.
called_from_lib:libQt6Core.so.6
called_from_lib:libQt6Gui.so.6
called_from_lib:libQt6Widgets.so.6
called_from_lib:libQt6Test.so.6
called_from_lib:libqoffscreen.so

# A report with a Qt frame in its stack is Qt's: its synchronisation is invisible here.
race:libQt6Core.so.6
race:libQt6Gui.so.6
race:libQt6Widgets.so.6
race:libQt6Test.so.6
race:libqoffscreen.so
mutex:libQt6Core.so.6
mutex:libQt6Gui.so.6
mutex:libQt6Widgets.so.6
mutex:libQt6Test.so.6
mutex:libqoffscreen.so
deadlock:libQt6Core.so.6
deadlock:libQt6Gui.so.6
deadlock:libQt6Widgets.so.6
deadlock:libQt6Test.so.6
deadlock:libqoffscreen.so
